{"depth":"full","paper_type":"archival_reuse","paper_type_evidence":[{"loc":"abstract","quote":"We utilize ALMA archival data to estimate the dust disk size of 152 protoplanetary disks in Lupus (1-3 Myr), Chamaeleon I (2-3 Myr), and Upper-Sco (5-11 Myr)."}],"fields":["protoplanetary_disks","solar_system"],"question":{"value":"How do dust disk sizes, and their relations to mm luminosity and stellar properties, differ among star-forming regions of different ages when derived homogeneously, and how does the Solar System's outer edge compare?","evidence":[{"loc":"abstract","quote":"We use these 199 homogeneously derived disk sizes to identify empirical disk-disk and disk-host property relations as well as to search for evolutionary trends."}]},"targets":[{"name":"Lupus disks (62 detections modelled; 50 retained)","details":[{"loc":"2.2 Lupus, Cha I, and USco","quote":"We include in our sample all the 62 detections from the ALMA project `2013.1.00220.S` (PI: Johnathan Williams) as presented in (cite: Ansdell2016)."}],"coordinates":"not stated","redshift_or_distance":"Gaia DR2 distances from Andrews2018a (~136-175 pc, Table 2)","distance_source":"Gaia DR2 as presented in Andrews2018a"},{"name":"Chamaeleon I disks (72 detections; 58 retained)","details":[{"loc":"2.2 Lupus, Cha I, and USco","quote":"We use the 11 detections reported in (cite: Long2018), giving us a total of 72 Cha I disks."}],"coordinates":"not stated","redshift_or_distance":"Bailer-Jones2018 Gaia DR2 distances; median 190 pc when unavailable","distance_source":"Bailer-Jones et al. 2018 Gaia catalogue"},{"name":"Upper Scorpius disks (50 detections; 44 retained)","details":[{"loc":"2.2 Lupus, Cha I, and USco","quote":"To estimate disk sizes we have selected those 50 USco targets that were detected."}],"coordinates":"not stated","redshift_or_distance":"Bailer-Jones2018 Gaia DR2 distances; median 144 pc when unavailable","distance_source":"Bailer-Jones et al. 2018 Gaia catalogue"}],"alma_project_codes":[{"code":"2013.1.00220.S","evidence":[{"loc":"2.2 Lupus, Cha I, and USco","quote":"We include in our sample all the 62 detections from the ALMA project `2013.1.00220.S` (PI: Johnathan Williams)"}]},{"code":"2013.1.00437.S","evidence":[{"loc":"2.2 Lupus, Cha I, and USco","quote":"from ALMA project `2013.1.00437.S` (PI: Pascucci, I.)"}]},{"code":"2015.1.00333.S","evidence":[{"loc":"2.2 Lupus, Cha I, and USco","quote":"were re-observed at 5 times higher sensitivity in project `2015.1.00333.S` (PI: Pascucci, I.)"}]},{"code":"2011.0.00526.S","evidence":[{"loc":"2.2 Lupus, Cha I, and USco","quote":"USco was observed in projects `2011.0.00526.S` and `2013.1.00395.S` (PI: Carpenter, J.)"}]},{"code":"2013.1.00395.S","evidence":[{"loc":"2.2 Lupus, Cha I, and USco","quote":"USco was observed in projects `2011.0.00526.S` and `2013.1.00395.S` (PI: Carpenter, J.)"}]}],"data_settings":[{"field":"bands","value":"Band 7 (~880-975 um)","evidence":[{"loc":"3 ALMA Observations and Data Reduction","quote":"We re-reduce band 7 ($\sim880-975\ensuremath{\mu}m$) observations of similar sensitivity for the sample of disks presented in Section [2.2](#sec_sample_alma)."}]},{"field":"angular_resolution","value":"Typical beams: Lupus 0.28-0.35", Cha I 0.5-0.7", USco 0.35-0.75"","evidence":[{"loc":"Table 1","quote":"| Lupus   | ALMA      |  935-954  | $0.28 - 0.35$ |     |     |"}]},{"field":"pi_or_archival_as_stated","value":"Archival ALMA data; 2011.0.00526.S measurement sets provided by PI/co-author J. Carpenter","evidence":[{"loc":"abstract","quote":"We utilize ALMA archival data to estimate the dust disk size of 152 protoplanetary disks"},{"loc":"3 ALMA Observations and Data Reduction","quote":"Measurement sets from the project `2011.0.00526.S` (USco) were directly provided by the PI and co-author J. Carpenter."}]}],"lines_targeted":[],"other_facilities":[{"name":"SMA","provided":{"value":"Oph and Tau/Aur disk sizes taken from literature (880 um)","evidence":[{"loc":"2 Initial Sample Selection","quote":"We use previously published disk sizes for Oph and Tau/Aur with observations obtained by the Submillimeter Array (SMA; see Section [2.1](#sec_sample_lit) for more details)"}]}},{"name":"Gaia DR2","provided":{"value":"Distances used to rescale stellar luminosities","evidence":[{"loc":"2.2.1 Stellar masses","quote":"Then, we scale these luminosities to the new Gaia DR2 distances."}]}}],"external_data_from_literature":[{"what":{"value":"Oph and Tau/Aur disk sizes (R68), Gaia-updated","evidence":[{"loc":"2.1 Oph and Tau/Aur","quote":"relying on the Gaia-updated values reported in (cite: Andrews2018a)"}]},"citation":"(cite: Tripathi2017); (cite: Andrews2018a)"},{"what":{"value":"Stellar Teff and bolometric luminosities for Lupus, Cha I, USco","evidence":[{"loc":"2.2.1 Stellar masses","quote":"For Lupus we rely on (cite: Alcala2014; Biazzo2017; Frasca2017; Andrews2018a); For Cha I on (cite: Manara2016; Manara2017); while for USco we rely on (cite: Barenfeld2016)."}]},"citation":"Alcala2014; Biazzo2017; Frasca2017; Andrews2018a; Manara2016; Manara2017; Barenfeld2016"},{"what":{"value":"Self-calibrated visibilities for the 10 brightest Cha I disks","evidence":[{"loc":"3 ALMA Observations and Data Reduction","quote":"for the 10 brightest Cha I disks[^1], we use self-calibrated visibilities from (cite: Pascucci2016)"}]},"citation":"(cite: Pascucci2016)"}],"simulations_or_models_as_data":[],"calibration":[{"field":"pipeline_or_manual","value":"NAASC-provided calibration scripts (phase, bandpass, flux) used to build measurement sets","evidence":[{"loc":"3 ALMA Observations and Data Reduction","quote":"measurement sets are built using the calibration scripts created by the North American ALMA Science Center (NAASC)."}]},{"field":"casa_version","value":"Same as NAASC scripts; CASA 4.7.2-el6 for averaging; 5.1.1-5.el7 for 2013.1.00220.S and A001_X11d_X13","evidence":[{"loc":"3 ALMA Observations and Data Reduction","quote":"Calibration of the sources in science goal `A001_X11d_X13` (Cha I) and project `2013.1.00220.S` (Lupus) was performed using `CASA` version `5.1.1-5.el7`."}]},{"field":"self_calibration","value":"Only for 10 brightest Cha I disks (from Pascucci2016); not applied elsewhere","evidence":[{"loc":"3 ALMA Observations and Data Reduction","quote":"The disk sizes resulting from self-calibrated visibilities are the same as those derived from non-self-calibrated visibilities, hence we do not apply self calibration to the other regions."}]}],"imaging":[],"inherited":[{"what":{"value":"Nuker-profile visibility modeling and effective-radius (R68) definition","evidence":[{"loc":"4 Modeling Method","quote":"To expand upon the results presented in (cite: Tripathi2017) and (cite: Andrews2018a), we use a similar method to derive disk sizes for Lupus, ChaI and USco."}]},"from_reference":"(cite: Tripathi2017); (cite: Andrews2018a)"},{"what":{"value":"Stellar masses via Bayesian inference approach","evidence":[{"loc":"2.2.1 Stellar masses","quote":"Stellar masses are determined following the Bayesian inference approach described in (cite: Pascucci2016)."}]},"from_reference":"(cite: Pascucci2016)"},{"what":{"value":"Dust mass: optically thin, 20 K","evidence":[{"loc":"6.2 The outer edge of the Solar System","quote":"Dust disk masses are estimated assuming optically thin emission and a fixed temperature of 20 K for the emitting grains as in the 20 K dust-mass calculation described in (cite: Pascucci2016)."}]},"from_reference":"(cite: Pascucci2016)"},{"what":{"value":"Lmm definition: flux density scaled to 140 pc","evidence":[{"loc":"Table 2 notes","quote":"For consistency, our definition of $L_{\text{mm}}$ is the same as found in (cite: Andrews2018a). It is a flux density scaled to 140 pc."}]},"from_reference":"(cite: Andrews2018a)"}],"unstated_but_needed":["Exact channel/time averaging per source","Weighting/robust and imaging parameters (visibility fitting only, uv-data weights)","Flux calibration uncertainty","Line-free channel selection / handling of CO line contamination in band 7","Exact Lmm conversion (flux integration, fluxes used) from Andrews2018a","Exact MCMC convergence criterion per source","Rule for choosing between resolved and unresolved model beyond reduced chi2","Cha I science goal A001_X11d_X13 project code not printed"],"analysis_steps":[{"order":1,"goal":"Reduce and average visibilities","method":{"value":"NAASC calibration scripts, remove 8 edge channels per spw, average in time and channel","evidence":[{"loc":"3 ALMA Observations and Data Reduction","quote":"Because the 8 outermost edge channels in each spectral window are typically noisy, we remove them from our measurement sets."}]},"software":["CASA"],"key_parameters":[{"value":"Spw averaging typically 19 channels (22 for 2011.0.00526.S); time averaging 2-30 s","evidence":[{"loc":"3 ALMA Observations and Data Reduction","quote":"However, we constrain all time averaging to be between 2 and 30 seconds."}]}],"assumptions":[],"fitting":{"value":"not stated","evidence":[]},"validation":{"value":"not stated","evidence":[]}},{"order":2,"goal":"Fit radial brightness profiles to visibilities","method":{"value":"Nuker profile and point source fit in visibility domain with GALARIO and emcee; also fit i, PA, offsets, lnwcorr","evidence":[{"loc":"4 Modeling Method","quote":"For all disks, we test two different functions for generating the radial profile shape: a Nuker profile (cite: Lauer1995) and a Dirac delta function (point source)."}]},"software":["GALARIO","emcee"],"key_parameters":[{"value":"70 walkers, 100,000 steps, burn-in 100 walkers x 1000 steps; uniform priors","evidence":[{"loc":"4 Modeling Method","quote":"We cover this parameter space with 70 chains (ensemble sample walkers) which individually take 100,000 steps in order to sample the posterior probability distribution function (PDF)."}]}],"assumptions":[{"value":"Axisymmetric disks","evidence":[{"loc":"4 Modeling Method","quote":"we assume axisymmetric disks and use a parametric radial intensity profile to fit the disk intensity in the visibility domain."}]}],"fitting":{"value":"emcee MCMC, uniform priors (Rt in [0.005,3], gamma [-11,4], logalpha [0.3,1.3], beta [1,17], logF0 [3,12], i [0,90], PA [0,179], offsets +-3 arcsec, lnwcorr [-10,10]); likelihood on real and imaginary visibilities","evidence":[{"loc":"4 Modeling Method","quote":"Uniform priors are used over a parameter space defined as: $\ensuremath{
